    "### GNN Model"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "68442a1f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Some considerations: what sampling scheme? What aggregation method? What research backs up our choice?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f30f3dcc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This section implements a **mini-batch sampled relation-aware R-GCN**.\n",
    "\n",
    "Rationale:\n",
    "- The graph contains multiple edge types with different semantic meanings.\n",
    "- `FastRGCNConv` allows the model to learn relation-specific transformations.\n",
    "- Mini-batch neighborhood sampling makes training feasible on a large user graph.\n",
    "\n",
    "Accordingly, we keep:\n",
    "- `edge_type` to represent **what kind of relation** an edge encodes\n",
    "- `edge_weight` only as an optional **strength within that relation**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5859e483",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Model choice: mini-batch sampled R-GCN\n",
    "\n",
    "The final model is a **two-layer relation-aware graph neural network** built with `FastRGCNConv`.\n",
    "\n",
    "Why this model is used here:\n",
    "- it is designed for **multi-relational graphs**\n",
    "- it preserves relation semantics directly through `edge_type`\n",
    "- it scales better than full-batch relational GNN training when paired with neighborhood sampling\n",
    "- it is more suitable than a homogeneous GraphSAGE baseline when the relation identity itself is informative\n",
    "\n",
    "Compared with a standard GraphSAGE formulation, this design better matches the structure of the current Twitter bot graph, where the distinction between follow, retweet, reply, quote, mention, and topic relations is important."
   ]
  },
  {
